<p>U.S. senators vote March 4 on a bipartisan war powers resolution that would require congressional authorization for hostilities against Iran—and would seek to end U.S. involvement without it. The push, backed by Democrats and a handful of Republicans, is meant to reassert Congress’ role in decisions about war. But supporters are widely expected to come up short of the simple majority needed to advance in the Senate.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/WpDLPx9BsCPEQVx1Y_C4qahlKzV7pA36lo2OI646RZQON4xU0ANf4mq4f2WUO08NHkAPGj1cuIXV5BST1oQ7TQsTldoXIGTnYgU1aBQ6OwIQ5AFhnSv0ECwQJltu3Y42tf8WIAnn5QFILsrUvbxkJMM72nwan9N8UEmJEF0jLDjXGvacKGIBjHtlPvWNPcdhRV07wygg3cHNYothIS9raBxtxnXQGcQ0OOVZNT_F2sh-IquzJ2vifhOaJTynQOYT8USOZ9K1igBnLygHeA' alt='Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer arrives to speak with reporters at the U.S. Capitol, March 3, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Two House tracks: Rep. Ro Khanna and Rep. Thomas Massie introduced a bipartisan resolution directing the president to pull U.S. forces from hostilities against Iran without congressional approval. A separate House effort led by Rep. Josh Gottheimer takes a more consultative route.</li><li>What the Senate resolution targets: Reuters describes the measure as an attempt to rein in what sponsors call a widening military campaign, including repeated deployments tied to the Iran conflict.</li><li>Democrats split on urgency: CBS News reported that some Democrats question how urgent the Iran strikes were as they weigh the administration’s rationale.</li><li>Politics and strategy in the background: Coverage of this week’s votes casts them as early signals of where lawmakers stand as debate grows over U.S. goals in the conflict—and the political fallout heading into midterm campaigns.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Sen. Tim Kaine (D-Va.): He says Congress should vote before the U.S. is at war, and argues lawmakers should return to Washington to vote given the risks to troops. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1978174/us-congress-considers-moves-to-curtail-trumps-war-powers'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Rep. Josh Gottheimer (D-N.J.) and House centrists: They favor a more cautious step that stops short of an immediate halt, stressing War Powers Act compliance and consultation with Congress. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1978174/us-congress-considers-moves-to-curtail-trumps-war-powers'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Democrats reacting to the strikes (as described by CBS News): Some Democrats have questioned how urgent the president’s Iran strikes were as Congress weighs war-powers measures. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/video/democrats-raise-doubts-about-urgency-of-trumps-iran-strikes/'>CBS News</a>)</li><li>Broader debate in Congress (as characterized in coverage): Reporting describes lawmakers weighing where the war is headed and pressing for clearer U.S. objectives as the consequences—and domestic political stakes—come into sharper view. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/iran-war-congress-taking-first-votes-as-debate-rages-about-us-goals/article70702646.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/iran-war-congress-taking-first-votes-as-debate-rages-about-us-goals/article70702646.ece'>Iran war: Congress taking first votes as debate rages about U.S. goals</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/us-lawmakers-set-vote-war-powers-iran-conflict-widens'>US lawmakers set to vote on war powers as Iran conflict widens</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/us-politics/article-congress-to-take-first-votes-on-iran-war-as-debate-rages-about-us/'>Congress to take first votes on Iran war as debate rages about U.S. goals</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/senate-vote-iran-war-powers-resolution-trump/'>Senate to vote on Trump&#x27;s war powers after attack on Iran</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a hr